/// United States federal income tax.
///
/// Everyone who `lives us` is under these laws, and so is everything they own.
/// A household is governed as one: its members' pay, gains and withholding
/// count into one return, and a household that files `joint` is taxed on the
/// sum. The laws below watch value arrive and leave and count what the
/// Internal Revenue Code calls income into named tallies. The return closes on
/// April 15 of the next year, and on that day the laws that figure it run: what
/// the journal recognizes for the year by then counts, so an installment paid in
/// January for the year before, or an IRA contribution made before the deadline,
/// is part of it.
///
/// The tallies are the lines of `axiom tax`, and they are the shared vocabulary
/// of every system under `us`. A system for a kind of account adds to them, a
/// state reads them, and a project counts into the ones marked *slot* to bring
/// a deduction, a credit or a tax this system does not know. The order laws run
/// in follows what they read, so no file name changes a tax bill:
///
///   wages                 gross pay, before anything is deferred
///   pretax                pay deferred or set aside before tax, taken back out
///   interest              interest earned outside a tax-deferred account
///   dividends             dividends paid outside a tax-deferred account
///   short-term-gains      gains on things held a year or less
///   long-term-gains       gains on things held longer than a year
///   distributions         taxable money that came out of a tax-deferred account
///   total-income          the sum of those, gains netted (Form 1040, line 9)
///   adjustments           slot: deductions above the line, taken from income
///   agi                   adjusted gross income: total income less adjustments
///   itemized              slot: itemized deductions, against the standard one
///   deductions            slot: the larger of the two, and any other deduction
///   taxable-income        agi less deductions
///   income-tax            the tax on it, on the ordinary and gains brackets
///   other-taxes           slot: taxes added to income tax (net investment
///                         income tax, self-employment tax)
///   credits               slot: what reduces the tax itself
///   total-tax             income tax and other taxes, less credits
///   payments              tax already paid: withheld from pay, or estimated
///                         `for` the year
///
/// What is owed is the total tax less the payments, and it is owed by April 15
/// of the next year. When more was paid than is due the amount is negative: the
/// IRS owes you a refund, and the report shows it as a negative amount owed.
///
/// Simplified on purpose. It has the standard deduction, itemizing, the child
/// tax credit and the net investment income tax, but not payroll tax, the
/// alternative minimum tax, phase-outs of deductions and credits, or carrying
/// a capital loss into the next year. Only the `single` and `joint` filing
/// statuses have figures.
///
/// The figures are community-maintained data. Verify them against the IRS
/// (irs.gov, "Revenue Procedures" for each year) before relying on them, and
/// correct them in a pull request when they are wrong.
system us

use std


// ─── Kinds and entities ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/// An account whose growth is not taxed while it stays inside: a 401(k), an
/// IRA, a 529 plan, an HSA. Tax falls due when value leaves for an account
/// that is not tax-deferred, so the laws of each kind decide what it costs.
///
/// What comes out is income unless it pays what the account is for: `pays`
/// names the kind of place that may take it tax-free (a 529 pays `education`, an
/// HSA `medical`). A kind that taxes what comes out by its own rules, as a Roth
/// IRA does, says `taxed-by-kind true`.
kind tax-deferred : asset
  deferred
  has pays          kind
  has taxed-by-kind bool
  has deductible    bool
  pays              tax-deferred
  taxed-by-kind     false
  deductible        false

  /// Money paid into an account whose contributions are deductible (a
  /// traditional IRA, an HSA, a SEP-IRA), from money that was taxed already, comes
  /// off income (IRC §219, §223(a), §404(h)). A payroll contribution was never
  /// taxed, so it is not deducted twice. The income limits on deducting an IRA
  /// contribution are not modelled. It counts for the account's owner: a person's
  /// own return, so on a joint return hold a deductible account in the household's
  /// name for its deduction to reach it.
  ///
  /// To fix: a contribution that is not deductible (Form 8606) is written with
  /// `basis` for its full amount, and marked `#nondeductible`.
  law deduct-contributions
    on in
    when self.deductible
    when from is asset and not from is tax-deferred
    when not flow is #nondeductible
    count amount as adjustments

/// Income tax withheld from pay, or paid to the IRS directly. Every arrival
/// counts as tax already paid for the year it is `for`.
kind federal-tax : expense

/// Health premiums, commuter benefits and anything else taken from pay before
/// tax (IRC §125). Pay counts whole, and what goes here comes back out of it.
kind pretax-benefit : expense

/// Interest on a loan secured by your home (IRC §163(h)): itemized.
kind mortgage-interest : expense

/// Tax on the home, paid to the county or from escrow (IRC §164): itemized,
/// with state income tax, up to a cap.
kind property-tax : expense

/// Gifts to a charity (IRC §170): itemized.
kind charity : expense

/// Your main home, held for your own use. A loss on it is not deductible, and
/// part of a gain on it is excluded when it is sold (IRC §121).
kind home : personal-use

/// The Internal Revenue Service.
entity irs : government


// ─── Counting income ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/// Wages are ordinary income (IRC §61(a)(1)). Everything that arrives from a
/// place of kind `wages` counts, wherever it lands: in checking, in a 401(k)
/// or in withholding, so the total is the gross pay on the pay stub.
///
/// To fix: if an arrival is not pay, book it under a place that is not of kind
/// `wages`.
law count-wages
  on in
  when from is wages
  count amount as wages
  count amount as total-income

/// Pay that goes into a tax-deferred account, or to a pre-tax benefit, is pay
/// you chose not to receive, so it is not taxed this year (IRC §125,
/// §402(e)(3)). The wages law counted it as pay; this law counts it as
/// deferred and takes it back out of income. Roth deferrals are not modelled.
///
/// To fix: pay that was taxed already is not pre-tax: send it from an account,
/// not from the paycheck.
law exclude-pretax
  on in
  when from is wages
  when to is tax-deferred | pretax-benefit
  count amount as pretax
  count -amount as total-income

/// Interest is ordinary income in the year it is earned (IRC §61(a)(4)),
/// except inside a tax-deferred account, which is taxed when money leaves it.
///
/// To fix: if it is not income, book it under a place that is not of kind
/// `interest`.
law count-interest
  on in
  when from is interest
  when not to is tax-deferred
  count amount as interest
  count amount as total-income
  count amount as investment-income

/// Dividends are income in the year they are paid (IRC §61(a)(7)), except
/// inside a tax-deferred account. All dividends are treated as ordinary; a
/// qualified dividend would be taxed at the lower capital-gains rates.
///
/// To fix: if it is not a dividend, book it under a place that is not of kind
/// `dividends`.
law count-dividends
  on in
  when from is dividends
  when not to is tax-deferred
  count amount as dividends
  count amount as total-income
  count amount as investment-income

/// A gain on something held for a year or less is taxed as ordinary income
/// (IRC §1222). A tax-deferred account realizes no gains of its own: what
/// leaves it is counted by the law for distributions. A loss on something you
/// keep for your own use is not deductible (IRC §165(c)).
///
/// To fix: if the sale was not a disposal, move the holding between your own
/// accounts instead of out of them.
law count-short-term-gains
  on gain
  when gain != empty
  when held <= 1y
  when not from is tax-deferred
  when gain > empty or not from is personal-use
  count gain as short-term-gains

/// A gain on something held for more than a year is taxed at the lower
/// capital-gains rates (IRC §1(h)), and a loss offsets other gains and up to
/// 3,000 USD of other income (IRC §1211). The sale of a home held two years or
/// more excludes the first 250,000 USD of gain, or 500,000 USD on a joint return
/// (IRC §121). Only how long it was held is tested, not that you lived there.
///
/// To fix: if the sale was not a disposal, move the holding between your own
/// accounts instead of out of them.
law count-long-term-gains
  on gain
  when gain != empty
  when held > 1y
  when not from is tax-deferred
  when gain > empty or not from is personal-use
  let excluded = if from is home and held >= 2y then min(gain, home-exclusion[year, owner.filing]) else empty
  count gain - excluded as long-term-gains

/// What comes out of a tax-deferred account is income in the year it comes out
/// (IRC §402(a), §408(d), §529(c)(3), §223(f)): the part that was never taxed,
/// which is the gain over what was put in. Paying what the account is for
/// (`pays`), or moving the money to another tax-deferred account, is not a
/// distribution, and neither is the market changing what the account is worth.
///
/// To fix: pay the expense straight from the account, or, if this is a
/// reimbursement, mark the withdrawal `!` with the reason, which waives the
/// penalty but not this income.
law count-distributions
  on gain
  when from is tax-deferred
  when gain > empty
  when not from.taxed-by-kind
  when not to is market | tax-deferred | from.pays
  count gain as distributions
  count gain as total-income

/// Tax withheld from pay, or paid in installments, is credited against the tax
/// owed for the year the payment is `for`, so one made in January for the year
/// before counts for that year.
///
/// To fix: send the payment to a place of kind `federal-tax`, and say what year
/// it is for with `for 2025`.
law count-payments
  on in
  when to is federal-tax
  count amount as payments


// ─── Itemizing ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/// Mortgage interest and gifts to charity are itemized deductions.
///
/// To fix: book the payment under a place of kind `mortgage-interest` or
/// `charity`, or count what a project knows into `itemized`.
law itemize-interest-and-gifts
  on in
  when to is mortgage-interest | charity
  count amount as itemized

/// Property tax is a state and local tax. So is state income tax, which the
/// state systems count when it is withheld or paid.
///
/// To fix: book the payment under a place of kind `property-tax`.
law count-property-tax
  on in
  when to is property-tax
  count amount as salt

/// State and local taxes are deducted only up to a cap (IRC §164(b)(6)).
///
/// To fix: the cap is a fact of the year, not a choice: itemize by other means
/// or take the standard deduction.
law cap-salt
  each year closing 04-15
  count min(tally(salt), salt-cap[year, owner.filing]) as itemized


// ─── The return ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/// Gains and losses are netted before they are taxed (IRC §1222): a loss of one
/// term first offsets the gain of the other, and only what is left counts. A
/// net loss reduces income by at most 3,000 USD, and the rest is not carried
/// into the next year here. Adjusted gross income is total income less the
/// adjustments projects and systems counted.
///
/// To fix: to change what counts, change what was counted: `axiom why agi`
/// lists every flow and law behind it.
law adjusted-gross-income
  each year closing 04-15
  let short = tally(short-term-gains)
  let long = tally(long-term-gains)
  let short-net = max(short + min(long, empty), empty)
  let long-net = max(long + min(short, empty), empty)
  let loss = min(max(-(short + long), empty), capital-loss-limit[year])
  count long-net as net-long-term-gains
  count short-net + long-net as investment-income
  count tally(total-income) + short-net + long-net - loss - tally(adjustments) as agi

/// The deduction is the larger of the standard deduction for the filing status
/// and what was itemized (IRC §63). A project adds another deduction, such as
/// the qualified business income deduction, by counting it into `deductions`.
///
/// To fix: set `filing` on the person or household, and count itemized amounts
/// into `itemized`.
law standard-or-itemized
  each year closing 04-15
  count max(standard-deduction[year, owner.filing], tally(itemized)) as deductions

/// A credit for each qualifying child under 17 (IRC §24). Set `children` on the
/// person or household. The phase-out above 200,000 USD of income (400,000 USD
/// joint) and the refundable limit are not modelled: the credit is treated as
/// reducing the tax whole.
///
/// To fix: `children` counts children, and does not age: change it when a child
/// turns 17.
law child-tax-credit
  each year closing 04-15
  count child-credit[year] * owner.children as credits

/// The net investment income tax is 3.8% of net investment income, or of the
/// income above a threshold if that is less (IRC §1411). Interest, dividends
/// and net gains count; a project counts rent or anything else passive into
/// `investment-income`.
///
/// To fix: income above the threshold is what triggers it: adjustments and
/// deductions lower it, and nothing else does.
law net-investment-income-tax
  each year closing 04-15
  let excess = max(tally(agi) - investment-threshold[year, owner.filing], empty)
  count 3.8% * min(tally(investment-income), excess) as other-taxes

/// Federal income tax for the year (IRC §1), figured from the tallies above.
///
/// Ordinary income is adjusted gross income without net long-term gains. It is
/// reduced by the deductions and taxed on the ordinary brackets. Long-term
/// gains are stacked on top of it and taxed on the capital-gains brackets. Other
/// taxes are added, and credits taken off, to give the total tax. What was paid
/// for the year is set against it, and the rest is owed by April 15 (Form 1040).
/// When more was paid than is due, the amount is negative: a refund.
///
/// To fix: the brackets depend on `filing`: set `filing single` or `filing joint`
/// on the person or household that lives here.
law federal-income-tax
  each year closing 04-15
  let deduction = tally(deductions)
  let ordinary-income = tally(agi) - tally(net-long-term-gains)
  let taxable = max(tally(agi) - deduction, empty)
  let taxable-ordinary = min(max(ordinary-income - deduction, empty), taxable)
  let ordinary-tax = progressive(ordinary[year, owner.filing], taxable-ordinary)
  let tax-through-gains = progressive(capital-gains[year, owner.filing], taxable)
  let tax-below-gains = progressive(capital-gains[year, owner.filing], taxable-ordinary)
  let liability = ordinary-tax + tax-through-gains - tax-below-gains
  let total = liability + tally(other-taxes) - tally(credits)
  count taxable as taxable-income
  count liability as income-tax
  count total as total-tax
  owe total - tally(payments) to irs by date(year + 1, 4, 15) as federal-income-tax


// ─── Figures ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Ordinary income brackets: the marginal rate above each threshold.
// Rev. Proc. 2023-34 (2024), Rev. Proc. 2024-40 (2025), Rev. Proc. 2025-32 (2026).
param ordinary
  2024 single       0 USD 10% |  11_600 USD 12% |  47_150 USD 22% | 100_525 USD 24% | 191_950 USD 32% | 243_725 USD 35% | 609_350 USD 37%
  2024 joint        0 USD 10% |  23_200 USD 12% |  94_300 USD 22% | 201_050 USD 24% | 383_900 USD 32% | 487_450 USD 35% | 731_200 USD 37%
  2025 single       0 USD 10% |  11_925 USD 12% |  48_475 USD 22% | 103_350 USD 24% | 197_300 USD 32% | 250_525 USD 35% | 626_350 USD 37%
  2025 joint        0 USD 10% |  23_850 USD 12% |  96_950 USD 22% | 206_700 USD 24% | 394_600 USD 32% | 501_050 USD 35% | 751_600 USD 37%
  2026 single       0 USD 10% |  12_400 USD 12% |  50_400 USD 22% | 105_700 USD 24% | 201_775 USD 32% | 256_225 USD 35% | 640_600 USD 37%
  // verify: the 2026 joint thresholds against Rev. Proc. 2025-32.
  2026 joint        0 USD 10% |  24_800 USD 12% | 100_800 USD 22% | 211_400 USD 24% | 403_550 USD 32% | 512_450 USD 35% | 768_700 USD 37%

// Long-term capital gains and qualified dividends: the rate by where the
// taxable income lands, stacked on top of ordinary income.
param capital-gains
  2024 single       0 USD 0%  |  47_025 USD 15% | 518_900 USD 20%
  2024 joint        0 USD 0%  |  94_050 USD 15% | 583_750 USD 20%
  2025 single       0 USD 0%  |  48_350 USD 15% | 533_400 USD 20%
  2025 joint        0 USD 0%  |  96_700 USD 15% | 600_050 USD 20%
  2026 single       0 USD 0%  |  49_450 USD 15% | 545_500 USD 20%
  // verify: the 2026 joint thresholds against Rev. Proc. 2025-32.
  2026 joint        0 USD 0%  |  98_900 USD 15% | 613_700 USD 20%

// The standard deduction. The 2025 figures include the increase in Public Law
// 119-21 (15,000 and 30,000 before it).
param standard-deduction
  2024 single 14_600 USD
  2024 joint  29_200 USD
  2025 single 15_750 USD
  2025 joint  31_500 USD
  2026 single 16_100 USD
  2026 joint  32_200 USD

// The cap on the state and local tax deduction (IRC §164(b)(6)), raised by
// Public Law 119-21 from 2025. Its phase-down above 500,000 USD is not modelled.
// verify: the 2026 figure.
param salt-cap
  2024 single 10_000 USD
  2024 joint  10_000 USD
  2025 single 40_000 USD
  2025 joint  40_000 USD
  2026 single 40_400 USD
  2026 joint  40_400 USD

// The child tax credit per child (IRC §24), raised by Public Law 119-21.
param child-credit
  2024 2_000 USD
  2025 2_200 USD
  2026 2_200 USD

// The gain on a home that is excluded from tax (IRC §121).
param home-exclusion
  2024 single 250_000 USD
  2024 joint  500_000 USD

// The most a net capital loss lowers other income in a year (IRC §1211(b)).
param capital-loss-limit
  2024 3_000 USD

// Where net investment income tax starts (IRC §1411(b)). Not indexed.
param investment-threshold
  2024 single 200_000 USD
  2024 joint  250_000 USD
